 "endonym": {
  "native": "ព្រះរាជាណាចក្រកម្ពុជា",
  "roman": "Preah Reacheanachak Kampuchea"
 },
 "capsule": "The modern Kingdom of Cambodia (ព្រះរាជាណាចក្រកម្ពុជា, Preah Reacheanachak Kampuchea) traces to independence from France, proclaimed by King Norodom Sihanouk on 9 November 1953, though the monarchy itself was interrupted for over two decades after General Lon Nol's 1970 coup created the Khmer Republic, followed by the Khmer Rouge genocide (1975-79) and Vietnamese-backed occupation. The kingdom was formally restored under a new constitution after the 1991 Paris Peace Accords and UN-supervised elections, with Sihanouk reinstated as king in September 1993. His son Norodom Sihamoni has reigned since 2004 as a constitutional monarch, while executive power has rested since the 1980s with the Cambodian People's Party, led by Hun Sen and, from 2023, his son Hun Manet.",
 "citations": [
